  "query_preview": "/SATURDAY THROUGH WEDNESDAY/...\nIssued at 339 PM CDT Wed Sep 23 2026\n\nBroad ridging will reside along the Texas Gulf Coast with a \ndeparting mid-level shortwave tracking east of Nebraska towards the \nMississippi Valley. Shortwave ridging with relatively strong height \nrises will overspread the Central Plains on Saturday. The resultant \nsubsidence will quell precipitation potential and foster a slight \nwarmup across the region. An elongated surface trough will settle \ninto our western zones and once again serve as the dividing line \nbetween much humid, much milder temperatures east and drier, warmer \nair to the west. At this time, believe any increased fire weather \nthreat should remain to the west across the Panhandle but this will \nneed monitored in the coming days. Dry weather will persist through \nthe weekend into Sunday as daytime highs climb into the 70s and 80s.\n\nDeeper troughing will settle into the Great Basin around the start \nof the work week and this will lead to ridge breakdown locally. \nWithin the southwesterly flow, an influx of moisture from the \neastern Pacific from Hurricane Polo should lift northeast through \nthe Desert Southwest and into the Central Plains. Ensemble guidance \nis in good agreement in painting precipitable water (PWATs) \nexceeding 150% of normal by Monday and Tuesday. As an upper trough \ncrosses the Northern Plains, rain and thunderstorms will increase \nahead of and behind a passing frontal boundary. Ensembles continue \nto paint high probabilities for heavy rain with 50-80%+ chance to \nexceed 0.50\" of rainfall in the late Monday/Tuesday time frame. \nDetails remain murky at best so continue to check later forecasts \nfor the latest thinking. This increased potential for rain and \nthunderstorms will coincide with cooler air as NBM inner-quartile \nvalues fall into the 60s for much of western Nebraska. This will be \nfollowed by a slow climb through the end of the next suggesting a \nreturn to more seasonable temperatures appears probable as we start \nOctober.",
